WEBVTT

00:00.400 --> 00:18.417
A proof kernel checks whether a formal proof term has the type representing its theorem. Osprey is a functional programming language with a kernel written in Osprey. We will follow a dependent function example through inference, reduction, and comparison, explaining each step as it appears.

00:19.267 --> 00:33.291
Terms are tagged values. A sort gives a type of types at a universe level. A variable refers to a binder, a named input. A function type records its input and result. Application supplies an argument.

00:34.141 --> 00:52.103
Inference computes a term's type. Reduction simplifies terms by computation. Two terms are definitionally equal when they reduce to the same normal form. The checker compares normal forms structurally, including every binder and universe argument. A difference is an error.

00:52.953 --> 01:12.327
The surface parser reads declarations like the identity theorem and a chained implication. An elaborator turns each into a kernel term. Identity and typed implication composition check. A declaration claiming q from only p is rejected. The elaborator invents no unknown terms.

01:13.177 --> 01:27.655
The run prints the accepted identity and the rejected variant. The kernel's reason names the terms that failed to match. Nothing is waved through because a theorem has a promising name. The rejection controls the acceptance.

01:28.505 --> 01:52.390
The implementation has moved beyond this introductory example. It checks inductive declarations, Lean's natural-number addition commutativity proof, and nine selected supporting lemmas from the OpenAI math release. Each of those nine also has a corrupted-proof control and a false-statement control. The library port is underway, with concrete results you can reproduce.
